NAME
    mod-dynamic-loader - browser source composition via m.mod.DynamicLoader

DESCRIPTION
    m.mod.DynamicLoader fetches an HTTP-accessible JSON source descriptor
    and its listed JavaScript or CSS files, assembles sources in descriptor
    order and optionally inserts the assembled code in the browser DOM.

    This is a jsc-built browser module, not the separately maintained
    lib/sys/js/dynamic-loader.lib.js library. It does not provide the old
    global loadModulesDynamically or loadLibraryDynamically functions.

SETUP
    Compile the dedicated descriptor with the existing jsc command:

        jsc lib/sys/js/core/modules-dynamic-loader.json output.js

    The output filename is chosen by the caller. Serve the result to the
    browser as a classic script:

        <script src="/assets/output.js"></script>

    It publishes m.mod.Queue and m.mod.DynamicLoader in the browser.

PUBLIC METHODS
    new m.mod.DynamicLoader()
        Construct a browser dynamic source loader.

    compose(manifest, {signal} = {})
        Return Promise<{kind, code}> where kind is "js" or "css".
        Fetch the descriptor and source files; assemble in declared order,
        regardless of download completion order. Does not execute code.

    load(manifest, {replace = null, signal, nonce} = {})
        Return Promise<Element>. Compose the sources, append a classic
        script or style element to document.head and return that element.
        If replace is an attached same-tag element, remove it after the
        new element has been appended. Incorrect replacement type rejects.

        The Promise reports successful fetching, composition and DOM
        insertion, NOT successful parsing/execution or correctness of the
        loaded bundle. The bundle and calling application are responsible
        for correctness, runtime errors, readiness and recovery.

    manifest
        URL string resolved against document.baseURI, constrained to the
        document origin. The descriptor lists ordered modules/name/file
        nodes. File paths are resolved relative to the descriptor URL.
        A descriptor must select at least one source and only one kind:
        JavaScript or CSS.

    signal
        Optional AbortSignal passed to fetch. Stopping the internal Queue
        does not automatically cancel already started HTTP requests.

    nonce
        Optional nonce assigned to the new script/style element. Under a
        nonce-based Content Security Policy, supply the nonce on EVERY
        script load or replacement; it is not copied from replace.

EXAMPLE
    With /demo/modules-js.json and its JavaScript sources served from
    the same origin, after output.js has loaded:

        const loader = new m.mod.DynamicLoader();

        const assembled = await loader.compose("/demo/modules-js.json");
        console.log(assembled.kind, assembled.code);

        const element = await loader.load
        (
          "/demo/modules-js.json",
          {nonce: "the-current-page-nonce"}
        );

        // Inserting an element is not proof that application code ran.
        // The application checks its own readiness or exported API.

    For replacement, pass the returned element back as replace:

        const next = await loader.load
        (
          "/demo/updated-js.json",
          {replace: element, nonce: "the-current-page-nonce"}
        );

ERRORS AND LIMITS
    Descriptor errors, disallowed paths, cross-origin requests, network
    failures, non-success HTTP status, and invalid replacement targets
    reject the corresponding Promise. A source download failure stops
    assembly and prevents a partial bundle from being reported as success.

    Browser CSP may prevent an inserted script from running without
    causing load() to reject. Runtime syntax errors, thrown exceptions,
    missing dependencies and application-level failures are not caught
    or reported by load(). No successful-execution guarantee exists.

    The loader does not reverse side effects from executed JavaScript.
    Removal of an old script element does not undo globals, timers,
    listeners or other effects. Replacement is not transactional and
    is not rollback-safe if the new script is blocked or fails at runtime.

    The loader is browser-only. A web server must serve the manifest and
    source files. The legacy dynamic-loader.lib.js manual remains a
    separate operational reference while that library is present.

